values ('c2d3e4f5-6a7b-4d92-9e12-3f4a5b6c7d22', 'contemporary-social-theory', 'Contemporary Social Theory', 'This opening topic frames theory as a toolkit for explaining social order, conflict, meaning, power, identity, and change. Contemporary theory grows from classical debates while revising their blind spots. Functionalism asks how social institutions create order, integration, and stability; conflict theory centers domination, exploitation, contradiction, and struggle; interactionist theory studies meaning-making in everyday life; and phenomenology and ethnomethodology ask how ordinary social reality becomes taken for granted. Critical theory links social analysis to emancipation by examining domination in capitalism, culture, reason, and modern institutions. Feminist theory analyzes gender as a social structure, identity, embodied experience, and field of power, and essential frameworks for race, coloniality, identity, and power make contemporary theory more complete. Poststructural and postmodern theory destabilize assumptions about truth, subjectivity, representation, and power. Bourdieu offers one of the strongest bridges between structure and agency. Globalization theory studies cross-border flows, inequalities, institutions, identities, and risks. Network and rational-choice approaches model social relations, incentives, ties, and strategic action. Students compare how different theories explain domination and consent, how people act within conditions they did not choose, how concepts connect to research design, and how to test theories against urgent social questions, then compare, synthesize, and critique theories in a sustained argument.');

