  'This opening topic frames theory as a toolkit for explaining social order, conflict, meaning, power, identity, and change. Contemporary theory grows from classical debates while revising their blind spots. Functionalism asks how social institutions create order, integration, and stability; conflict theory centers domination, exploitation, contradiction, and struggle; interactionist theory studies meaning-making in everyday life; and phenomenology and ethnomethodology ask how ordinary social reality becomes taken for granted. Critical theory links social analysis to emancipation by examining domination in capitalism, culture, reason, and modern institutions. Feminist theory analyzes gender as a social structure, identity, embodied experience, and field of power, and essential frameworks for race, coloniality, identity, and power make contemporary theory more complete. Poststructural and postmodern theory destabilize assumptions about truth, subjectivity, representation, and power. Bourdieu offers one of the strongest bridges between structure and agency. Globalization theory studies cross-border flows, inequalities, institutions, identities, and risks. Network and rational-choice approaches model social relations, incentives, ties, and strategic action. Students compare how different theories explain domination and consent, how people act within conditions they did not choose, how concepts connect to research design, and how to test theories against urgent social questions, then compare, synthesize, and critique theories in a sustained argument.',
  $costjson${"slug": "contemporary-social-theory", "title": "Contemporary Social Theory", "description": "This opening topic frames theory as a toolkit for explaining social order, conflict, meaning, power, identity, and change. Contemporary theory grows from classical debates while revising their blind spots. Functionalism asks how social institutions create order, integration, and stability; conflict theory centers domination, exploitation, contradiction, and struggle; interactionist theory studies meaning-making in everyday life; and phenomenology and ethnomethodology ask how ordinary social reality becomes taken for granted. Critical theory links social analysis to emancipation by examining domination in capitalism, culture, reason, and modern institutions. Feminist theory analyzes gender as a social structure, identity, embodied experience, and field of power, and essential frameworks for race, coloniality, identity, and power make contemporary theory more complete. Poststructural and postmodern theory destabilize assumptions about truth, subjectivity, representation, and power. Bourdieu offers one of the strongest bridges between structure and agency. Globalization theory studies cross-border flows, inequalities, institutions, identities, and risks. Network and rational-choice approaches model social relations, incentives, ties, and strategic action. Students compare how different theories explain domination and consent, how people act within conditions they did not choose, how concepts connect to research design, and how to test theories against urgent social questions, then compare, synthesize, and critique theories in a sustained argument.", "topics": [{"id": "42dc4e1e-955c-49c3-a235-4a3b43228b85", "type": "topic", "title": "What Contemporary Social Theory Does", "description": "This opening topic frames theory as a toolkit for explaining social order, conflict, meaning, power, identity, and change. Students learn to read theory as argument, vocabulary, method, and critique.", "children": [{"id": "035529ab-4771-4f6f-8fda-bd985db7e3ca", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory as explanation and critique", "children": [{"id": "bb98fa86-a7d0-4a9b-aa6f-d74bf0c27800", "type": "concept", "title": "Concepts, mechanisms, and claims"}, {"id": "39746c1b-cdfc-4598-be42-1f78d5d92820", "type": "concept", "title": "Normative critique and empirical explanation"}, {"id": "a1af6ab2-9218-47df-9f86-15bb1d284005", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as a lens rather than an answer key"}], "description": "Students learn why theory matters for seeing social patterns that common sense often hides."}, {"id": "00ee27a6-32b5-4c1d-9b7c-a3fedff8304b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading difficult theory", "children": [{"id": "2a30a067-f079-4d47-b3fe-0e11a9439234", "type": "concept", "title": "Key terms and conceptual maps"}, {"id": "345fbd92-916e-4e10-8c80-bcda6ec5e657", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument reconstruction"}, {"id": "e4c583d9-8818-41e3-a9b2-73ae2b9d9a59", "type": "concept", "title": "Passage annotation and seminar questions"}], "description": "Theory becomes manageable when students learn how to track terms, arguments, assumptions, and stakes."}, {"id": "33afff2a-31df-47a7-8977-8ad285a6db0a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory and contemporary problems", "children": [{"id": "05516230-736e-4a16-b3f8-212d11f54de4", "type": "concept", "title": "Inequality, identity, and institutions"}, {"id": "fe93a1d4-cf22-4eea-9293-e2091490fc02", "type": "concept", "title": "Media, culture, and technology"}, {"id": "7289e55a-aac1-457d-be22-78a8d5c7fb1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Globalization, climate, and social change"}], "description": "Students connect abstract frameworks to concrete social issues."}]}, {"id": "ce2b4f28-36d5-41c9-9d69-bc38d6ba06c9", "type": "topic", "title": "Classical Foundations and Contemporary Problems", "description": "Contemporary theory grows from classical debates while revising their blind spots. This section links Marx, Durkheim, Weber, Simmel, Du Bois, Martineau, and Gilman to modern theoretical problems.", "children": [{"id": "c3f41393-ea6e-4354-81c6-14d53ee4444c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classical continuities", "children": [{"id": "edc5c207-f97e-41fd-9d67-3fe7621ddd01", "type": "concept", "title": "Marx and capitalism"}, {"id": "e129f39c-5999-4873-81dc-bf10b9ad1ed8", "type": "concept", "title": "Durkheim and social integration"}, {"id": "c394f0c1-d482-4d9d-bb96-c238edbb5605", "type": "concept", "title": "Weber and rationalization"}], "description": "Students see how contemporary theory inherits problems of capitalism, solidarity, rationalization, modernity, race, gender, and power."}, {"id": "13470aaf-4a73-4ba2-9a95-6d0555bd5ee0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Recovering excluded theorists", "children": [{"id": "ea8f87cb-618a-4235-84bd-18903e9cc3a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Du Bois and the color line"}, {"id": "5cea9c07-30f0-49bd-88e3-2173aecf9af5", "type": "concept", "title": "Martineau and gendered method"}, {"id": "9a30fb90-e3f3-4dae-8dbf-4c5ef4ac4d14", "type": "concept", "title": "Gilman and political economy of gender"}], "description": "A world-class theory course treats Du Bois, Martineau, Gilman, and other marginalized thinkers as central rather than supplemental."}, {"id": "d83c378a-499e-4497-ad07-8ce1593468ef", "type": "subtopic", "title": "From classical to contemporary", "children": [{"id": "38501f4a-9871-4f68-ad85-94a0a323891f", "type": "concept", "title": "Modernity and its critics"}, {"id": "3128982d-9e1b-4960-bffb-e1d76b0d3037", "type": "concept", "title": "Structure versus agency"}, {"id": "968412d6-d3de-4700-95a4-4740f2dfc37e", "type": "concept", "title": "Macro, meso, and micro levels"}], "description": "Students learn how later theorists keep, reject, or transform classical assumptions."}]}, {"id": "615ed7ac-33c2-4b35-b177-09c2209571f4", "type": "topic", "title": "Structural Functionalism and Social Systems", "description": "Functionalism asks how social institutions create order, integration, and stability. Students study both the power and limits of system-level explanation.", "children": [{"id": "dc7a58a7-a6c1-4721-8370-09903f5c5cc5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parsons and social order", "children": [{"id": "ed080a24-78a5-456f-9d3d-453a76187ccb", "type": "concept", "title": "AGIL framework"}, {"id": "020f6dd3-0c33-4e0a-ad1c-e561e94e0d52", "type": "concept", "title": "Role expectations"}, {"id": "be360b0a-c2ce-430d-9f92-0ba6ebed49e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Value consensus"}], "description": "Parsons helps students understand society as interdependent systems of norms, roles, and institutions."}, {"id": "7d5bc6ff-98c4-47b4-bd3d-e314b54af46c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Merton and middle-range theory", "children": [{"id": "628c9ef2-d586-456c-9c72-8bffc90397a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Manifest and latent functions"}, {"id": "b0b45e49-c889-4e26-bef9-7f10217ce34e", "type": "concept", "title": "Dysfunctions"}, {"id": "725a9131-686d-4161-ad46-176ccb164494", "type": "concept", "title": "Middle-range explanation"}], "description": "Merton makes functional analysis more precise by focusing on unintended consequences and measurable social processes."}, {"id": "3c0c889a-ae21-41f9-9630-d61b73de2e16", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critiques of functionalism", "children": [{"id": "b54de1a5-4e96-40de-b0f3-f6078586179d", "type": "concept", "title": "Conservatism critique"}, {"id": "215a280f-b422-4c59-a75e-9948344206b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Power and inequality blind spots"}, {"id": "f996e41c-ed7f-46fa-9ff2-357b4416e47e", "type": "concept", "title": "Static versus dynamic models"}], "description": "Students learn why functionalism can understate conflict, domination, and historical change."}]}, {"id": "2ab69be3-b0c8-4d86-8a8d-6b7a659ffd56", "type": "topic", "title": "Conflict Theory and Neo-Marxism", "description": "Conflict theory centers domination, exploitation, contradiction, and struggle. Students learn to analyze capitalism, class, state power, ideology, and social change through neo-Marxist and critical traditions.", "children": [{"id": "a5e2d7fe-7880-4baf-948d-0085d6f633b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Capitalism and class power", "children": [{"id": "14e56898-02c0-4d2a-913e-6f00d791c63a", "type": "concept", "title": "Exploitation and surplus"}, {"id": "99a7675c-3b5a-47af-96fe-77172462b17b", "type": "concept", "title": "Class formation"}, {"id": "7bb328f8-6ae8-4a24-8d26-240cc4a983a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor, precarity, and commodification"}], "description": "Students revisit capitalism as a social relation, not just an economic system."}, {"id": "2b590db4-29c9-45b0-a22d-b9e35f355912", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Neo-Marxist revisions", "children": [{"id": "7332f6a4-4a49-4720-89ae-91de172c816d", "type": "concept", "title": "Gramsci and hegemony"}, {"id": "f0a04874-64da-4e56-93ba-fa9dcc9097a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Althusser and ideology"}, {"id": "e57311f0-c77b-4dc1-83ff-a05bdbeeb4ea", "type": "concept", "title": "World-systems and dependency thinking"}], "description": "Neo-Marxist theorists extend class analysis into culture, the state, ideology, and global capitalism."}, {"id": "fcd640f3-795c-41ca-843b-f48a0cc609f6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conflict beyond class reduction", "children": [{"id": "4a243dc0-4b09-4ab6-87a0-ca087256e0f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Racial capitalism"}, {"id": "ad479a67-3dd0-4e69-8b13-2fffc252ad63", "type": "concept", "title": "Social reproduction"}, {"id": "3a809887-7a80-417d-ae35-aa956f39d85f", "type": "concept", "title": "Ecological Marxism"}], "description": "Students examine how class interacts with race, gender, nation, empire, and environment."}]}, {"id": "1b3ddd92-4266-4965-bea5-05c4a9ccdb60", "type": "topic", "title": "Symbolic Interactionism and Dramaturgy", "description": "Interactionist theory studies meaning-making in everyday life. Students learn how selves, identities, situations, and social realities are produced through interaction.", "children": [{"id": "2e107e61-7776-4a5b-9b79-2dfedfb92c84", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mead and the social self", "children": [{"id": "ec2c654f-ee58-4e2a-9924-86ef628af7ab", "type": "concept", "title": "I and me"}, {"id": "490771eb-6ee5-427c-8eae-d4266e961b00", "type": "concept", "title": "Generalized other"}, {"id": "b05c6642-cfc6-4976-b3b6-d2eaa300c08b", "type": "concept", "title": "Mind, self, and society"}], "description": "Mead shows how the self emerges through communication, role-taking, and social interaction."}, {"id": "b286366e-459c-4850-96b2-71d41b138556", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Blumer and symbolic interactionism", "children": [{"id": "9332ad9f-a92e-4da3-8048-6aa4b5a06847", "type": "concept", "title": "Meaning, language, and interpretation"}, {"id": "7df0a07c-62d0-4dd9-b8b1-662f993c031c", "type": "concept", "title": "Joint action"}, {"id": "e3ab8fd8-a22d-4c23-9c52-9172cce97447", "type": "concept", "title": "Situational definitions"}], "description": "Blumer emphasizes that people act based on meanings formed and revised in interaction."}, {"id": "f5f45365-9b2b-42a0-b4cb-0a91ed1929d6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Goffman and dramaturgy", "children": [{"id": "913992de-aa96-481d-8299-c0735bf401a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Frontstage and backstage"}, {"id": "57692150-3d93-468c-8a51-860b3b839cfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Impression management"}, {"id": "d94ec48f-42ac-499d-b509-5eabf5d0ee2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Stigma and total institutions"}], "description": "Goffman gives students tools for analyzing performance, stigma, face-work, and institutional interaction."}]}, {"id": "409cc6e8-7827-4cf3-bd71-0d611a3fc465", "type": "topic", "title": "Phenomenology, Ethnomethodology, and Everyday Order", "description": "Phenomenology and ethnomethodology ask how ordinary social reality becomes taken for granted. Students study background knowledge, rules, interactional repair, and the production of everyday order.", "children": [{"id": "c80ad5b2-b946-4094-8d7f-66439a10c798", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Phenomenology of the social world", "children": [{"id": "21a8353f-e12e-4d0c-ae38-97ebe550b78a", "type": "concept", "title": "Lifeworld"}, {"id": "c53d2f64-15c2-49a7-875f-d25807b43573", "type": "concept", "title": "Typification"}, {"id": "39699486-8234-41e1-95de-2b10f27fdc4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersubjectivity"}], "description": "Students examine lived experience and common-sense knowledge as foundations of social reality."}, {"id": "8914af06-6ced-40fa-9f85-487a75ca1820", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnomethodology", "children": [{"id": "b1e7f560-2530-4d5d-89b6-63b80f62071c", "type": "concept", "title": "Indexicality"}, {"id": "1530aed3-3b07-4f4e-baa5-96918008a782", "type": "concept", "title": "Accountability"}, {"id": "ca6d1b4d-20e1-4f86-a1c6-c39921ee339d", "type": "concept", "title": "Breaching experiments"}], "description": "Ethnomethodology reveals the hidden methods people use to make situations intelligible."}, {"id": "b021ca87-8087-4788-a928-92b52b4e13a3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conversation and interaction order", "children": [{"id": "3505481e-55d7-4374-9067-1349286deb26", "type": "concept", "title": "Turn-taking"}, {"id": "901ef37f-945f-499d-a2be-15ecba6ef8ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Repair and sequence"}, {"id": "a24d4356-6a41-4b9a-9514-941f5e2bfa53", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional talk"}], "description": "Small-scale interaction becomes a window into norms, power, and institutional routines."}]}, {"id": "733d40b6-0ac1-4d6f-ad01-c7d11fb6b8f4", "type": "topic", "title": "Frankfurt School and Critical Theory", "description": "Critical theory links social analysis to emancipation by examining domination in capitalism, culture, reason, and modern institutions. Students learn to ask how society produces consent and constraint.", "children": [{"id": "b53be017-72cd-423c-ae3f-44dd6e57bf94", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early Frankfurt School", "children": [{"id": "b1b70303-eed5-480c-bbc1-b6da566baa69", "type": "concept", "title": "Horkheimer and Adorno"}, {"id": "9ae66bb3-03ce-44a4-9f50-879481792d08", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture industry"}, {"id": "aa824c3c-645f-47b4-9b26-ea5334c8050a", "type": "concept", "title": "Authoritarianism and mass society"}], "description": "Students study how theorists analyzed capitalism, culture, authority, and fascism."}, {"id": "e2366fc0-a913-4cf9-ae61-c474958d0a9d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reason, domination, and emancipation", "children": [{"id": "0d764598-7783-4e34-ab40-887d03f5b2a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Instrumental reason"}, {"id": "eddebc9f-b342-42d5-897c-21ca3c55ac8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Negative dialectics"}, {"id": "6b7830d7-f0cd-4e76-871a-38935d1f8ad0", "type": "concept", "title": "Immanent critique"}], "description": "Critical theory asks whether modern reason liberates people or becomes a tool of control."}, {"id": "bc852de1-142a-4f89-aead-9c4086d68940", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Habermas and communicative action", "children": [{"id": "19ea6297-6251-4231-b969-e1b3a8e52c48", "type": "concept", "title": "Lifeworld and system"}, {"id": "89974c6a-6af6-4720-98fd-93ecff497aaf", "type": "concept", "title": "Public sphere"}, {"id": "81730e96-6f46-4742-8f7f-a91f77f17b5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Discourse ethics"}], "description": "Habermas shifts attention to communication, public sphere, legitimacy, and democratic deliberation."}]}, {"id": "f5ad5db8-3d67-48f3-839d-3a34e07693b4", "type": "topic", "title": "Feminist Theory, Gender, and Sexuality", "description": "Feminist theory analyzes gender as a social structure, identity, embodied experience, and field of power. Students learn how feminist frameworks transform assumptions about knowledge, labor, family, sexuality, and inequality.", "children": [{"id": "e870ecc3-2125-4d77-bc59-870973cf26b3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist standpoint and epistemology", "children": [{"id": "0d05257d-0f80-4ca3-a831-7c9b2771ca4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Standpoint theory"}, {"id": "516e2150-806c-4019-b12a-ee86ba39d0af", "type": "concept", "title": "Situated knowledge"}, {"id": "088b9c0c-a606-48f5-9bfa-4ea3423abfc8", "type": "concept", "title": "Objectivity and reflexivity"}], "description": "Students examine how social location shapes what can be known and whose knowledge counts."}, {"id": "6a33cbf3-c457-4ba6-a9ee-7052b7a7cf64", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender, patriarchy, and social reproduction", "children": [{"id": "bd98d200-8132-4852-8d81-e39d16f4f47d", "type": "concept", "title": "Care work"}, {"id": "5df4bb11-dc56-4376-ac15-701fe095867f", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered organizations"}, {"id": "21600d0e-4bc1-48b6-b37d-e653fbb58d24", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment and discipline"}], "description": "Feminist theory shows how unpaid labor, family, institutions, and bodies reproduce social order."}, {"id": "5580e55e-68d0-44e4-a5d6-1e1f1a91c94b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Queer and sexuality theory", "children": [{"id": "c6086b4d-6819-444e-9d2e-989ef49a6407", "type": "concept", "title": "Heteronormativity"}, {"id": "329b30b7-5065-4fd4-9bb0-17645336f645", "type": "concept", "title": "Performativity"}, {"id": "108a2f41-5bad-4878-be19-7073c9cf1804", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual citizenship"}], "description": "Queer theory challenges fixed categories and examines norms governing desire, identity, and legitimacy."}]}, {"id": "af87c0b9-0543-4b21-9790-2985e8318f28", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Race, Intersectionality, and Decolonial Theory", "description": "This topic adds essential frameworks for race, coloniality, identity, and power that make contemporary theory more complete. Students learn to analyze overlapping systems rather than isolated categories.", "children": [{"id": "38837697-6fa0-4e36-82eb-7f04ca5f61db", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critical race and racial formation", "children": [{"id": "651a3b9a-3333-440d-80ff-931d475b1995", "type": "concept", "title": "Racialization"}, {"id": "94537217-9c15-41b1-990b-082c63bdab82", "type": "concept", "title": "Colorblindness and racial ideology"}, {"id": "f8db10ab-dd97-4d80-844c-d0d909579e08", "type": "concept", "title": "Racial capitalism"}], "description": "Students examine race as historically produced, institutionally embedded, and politically contested."}, {"id": "da022bd5-d286-49e9-835a-8627778e7b86", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intersectionality", "children": [{"id": "912d4c12-cab5-4027-9412-f08594738477", "type": "concept", "title": "Matrix of domination"}, {"id": "41c5f4b3-2abd-43a2-81c9-5ecac96e164c", "type": "concept", "title": "Interlocking oppression"}, {"id": "5ce848d5-5bb7-4a3e-93df-aff1e651cb27", "type": "concept", "title": "Patricia Hill Collins and Kimberle Crenshaw"}], "description": "Intersectionality studies how systems of power combine to shape lived experience and institutional outcomes."}, {"id": "186b52ef-147a-49db-bb45-65257030e48a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postcolonial and decolonial critique", "children": [{"id": "626d5ee7-3e1d-4c81-8536-a8d28d8da279", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism"}, {"id": "b7d4d0f1-e6ad-49e8-8afd-a1e2021eaa12", "type": "concept", "title": "Coloniality of power"}, {"id": "b38e46d6-699a-482c-b261-b0fdecf39a62", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemic justice and decolonizing knowledge"}], "description": "These frameworks challenge Eurocentric knowledge and examine colonial power after formal empire."}]}, {"id": "46b105e3-90ec-4132-bd74-ee30dd0e014d", "type": "topic", "title": "Poststructuralism and Postmodern Social Theory", "description": "Poststructural and postmodern theory destabilize assumptions about truth, subjectivity, representation, and power. Students learn the value and risks of theories that question grand narratives.", "children": [{"id": "c0f1f284-2b1d-4429-add8-78a16652a717", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Foucault on power and knowledge", "children": [{"id": "a59ccb3d-e2bc-4db2-8ac3-bbdf40ceb04a", "type": "concept", "title": "Discipline and surveillance"}, {"id": "65fd9f2e-506d-4875-b215-7f0bb30f65ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Biopower and governmentality"}, {"id": "87c313ea-bef0-47db-8788-5b08ee508892", "type": "concept", "title": "Genealogy and discourse"}], "description": "Foucault helps students analyze how institutions produce subjects, categories, and truths."}, {"id": "7470f504-7d65-4da3-9a61-4a83e74b0e8c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Baudrillard and simulation", "children": [{"id": "bb0a4a1b-7cb9-477e-a0b5-704379b7acf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Simulacra"}, {"id": "f006bd0f-9d9f-459d-b4f7-944a0590ccf9", "type": "concept", "title": "Consumer society"}, {"id": "a5654b8f-1a00-40e8-a2f9-d051685b80a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Media saturation"}], "description": "Baudrillard gives tools for thinking about media, signs, consumption, and hyperreality."}, {"id": "0db37144-feba-4685-82ac-dc8e6374c31e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postmodern critique", "children": [{"id": "de2691d6-e93a-4beb-8f65-b90c936ca807", "type": "concept", "title": "Grand narratives"}, {"id": "cba1e76a-de14-4256-9aa6-a6829bed0198", "type": "concept", "title": "Fragmentation"}, {"id": "62d43b63-b5b3-4e52-be7d-c74b17580d33", "type": "concept", "title": "Relativism debates"}], "description": "Students evaluate critiques of universality, progress, objectivity, and stable identity."}]}, {"id": "6d95737f-8a3c-4a99-b25c-dd1e1188ba94", "type": "topic", "title": "Bourdieu: Habitus, Capital, Field, and Practice", "description": "Bourdieu offers one of the strongest bridges between structure and agency. Students learn how dispositions, resources, fields, and symbolic power reproduce inequality through ordinary practice.", "children": [{"id": "0eeae38a-5116-4953-b0a7-e1ae409ab05f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Habitus and practice", "children": [{"id": "d94b7d25-7893-4d24-a22f-652d7e9dedcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Disposition"}, {"id": "1095a4e6-99d7-4fcd-bb4f-e33ad9284376", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical mastery"}, {"id": "538578f5-bb40-4bec-abb3-3069c4818210", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment and taste"}], "description": "Habitus explains how social history becomes embodied as durable tendencies and practical sense."}, {"id": "f5478beb-ffe3-41a6-85a5-1cf1b73b2f9e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Forms of capital", "children": [{"id": "77df4972-80b4-4a13-b306-26ac5632574f", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic capital"}, {"id": "dd58e412-fa0c-47d8-ad3c-60fc90e3e9d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural and social capital"}, {"id": "69dfd9db-8578-41e2-9d0a-0dc61943a86f", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbolic capital"}], "description": "Bourdieu extends capital beyond money to show how advantage travels through culture, networks, and recognition."}, {"id": "163bba07-4eb1-4628-bd9d-84245910b878", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fields and symbolic power", "children": [{"id": "fcf21666-4e79-45c0-84f2-2f25efb7f619", "type": "concept", "title": "Autonomy and heteronomy"}, {"id": "75c52a04-1c75-484b-b0ee-b8ca6f8d214d", "type": "concept", "title": "Doxa and misrecognition"}, {"id": "27e8e2eb-10c8-4532-a696-59209c982fd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Education, art, and class reproduction"}], "description": "Fields help students analyze arenas of struggle with their own rules, stakes, and hierarchies."}]}, {"id": "6ced9f1e-9f31-4e50-88c3-8a7394cd9030", "type": "topic", "title": "Globalization, World-Systems, and Transnational Social Life", "description": "Globalization theory studies cross-border flows, inequalities, institutions, identities, and risks. Students learn to analyze how local life is shaped by global systems.", "children": [{"id": "f820cc33-2697-469f-9851-71db20809c85", "type": "subtopic", "title": "World-systems and global capitalism", "children": [{"id": "a0b594e5-32d4-4ef0-96b4-16e0444be76b", "type": "concept", "title": "Core-periphery hierarchy"}, {"id": "6ea48ac4-f9ee-4371-99a1-530509d95fa6", "type": "concept", "title": "Unequal exchange"}, {"id": "d425b794-3e2f-453b-a682-f76e255bff1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Dependency and development"}], "description": "Students examine core, periphery, and semi-periphery as relational positions in global political economy."}, {"id": "5b232a06-b7f6-45e7-a6cd-beee0a004086", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Transnationalism and migration", "children": [{"id": "4acff12c-18b3-4ba8-be34-6bacccc2a162", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora"}, {"id": "f8c3f42d-35f3-414f-b9de-2b2c0d680ada", "type": "concept", "title": "Global care chains"}, {"id": "9067b4e8-0dc0-43e7-b58d-4a5a3345120e", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship and belonging"}], "description": "Globalization is lived through movement, remittances, diasporas, and border regimes."}, {"id": "4f5faf4e-9e05-4bf2-b10f-d7881d81da85", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global culture and risk", "children": [{"id": "890cd8df-dcc4-4634-a70f-1c70186e0663", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural homogenization and hybridity"}, {"id": "1330ddc9-3684-4fdd-8f46-6a0915e9fdcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk society"}, {"id": "52e6d6c6-ec09-420c-8a9b-fbfe7948addd", "type": "concept", "title": "Global governance"}], "description": "Students analyze global media, institutions, climate, pandemics, and shared vulnerabilities."}]}, {"id": "1045a563-0692-4ebb-aebc-a425bb169e2c", "type": "topic", "title": "Network Theory, Rational Choice, and Social Capital", "description": "Network and rational-choice approaches model social relations, incentives, ties, and strategic action. Students learn where formal and relational theories clarify social life and where they oversimplify it.", "children": [{"id": "c720c262-19ff-4bc4-9375-8901b602da47", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social network analysis", "children": [{"id": "8e8d0f6f-179c-4cdf-b4ce-253ee7d0a56b", "type": "concept", "title": "Strong and weak ties"}, {"id": "8b428f37-f453-4a91-8fc3-ba6670ca302d", "type": "concept", "title": "Centrality and brokerage"}, {"id": "58be1206-fa78-4123-88a4-b2a1a748d9ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Diffusion and contagion"}], "description": "Networks show how position, ties, and structure shape opportunity and behavior."}, {"id": "57a9dbf1-921d-4b72-83b5-e9e2f0cac751", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rational choice and exchange", "children": [{"id": "508f5186-a7c1-49bd-9e51-a57a8c6287eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Preferences and constraints"}, {"id": "4fbb6e1b-7507-426b-a090-d1e9106ff034", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective action"}, {"id": "41ea780d-0f23-4b30-8590-349df5c14674", "type": "concept", "title": "Game-like social situations"}], "description": "Rational-choice theory explains social action through incentives, constraints, and strategic decision-making."}, {"id": "f5982830-0fec-4e70-9514-8fdd72dee07f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social capital debates", "children": [{"id": "a22099b7-e0fb-4096-aa41-d2180ed88319", "type": "concept", "title": "Bonding and bridging capital"}, {"id": "6b046bd9-e315-4a76-b19e-96a74256aa97", "type": "concept", "title": "Trust and reciprocity"}, {"id": "14d90beb-ba07-4cb5-9c29-39023961c394", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of capital metaphors"}], "description": "Social capital links networks to trust, resources, inequality, and civic life."}]}, {"id": "a5803091-7657-42c1-9f22-70be661eb674", "type": "topic", "title": "Power, Ideology, Discourse, and Knowledge", "description": "This integrative topic helps students compare how different theories explain domination and consent. It connects Marxist ideology, Gramscian hegemony, Foucauldian discourse, feminist standpoint, and critical race epistemology.", "children": [{"id": "c11226f8-e50b-461e-a0c7-34fe6664a727", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ideology and hegemony", "children": [{"id": "a4c672ef-03b2-41be-86ee-516f492500d6", "type": "concept", "title": "False consciousness debates"}, {"id": "883ea566-bad8-458f-8c1e-d30cc09060e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Hegemony and common sense"}, {"id": "1ae139dd-ee8f-49d2-9872-428bc7c94501", "type": "concept", "title": "Media, education, and institutions"}], "description": "Students learn how domination can operate through common sense and consent, not only coercion."}, {"id": "b1bd621d-435b-48b5-886e-d003c5a73dac", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Discourse and subject formation", "children": [{"id": "4274c21c-c4d6-4bd0-8995-0c0e416a5617", "type": "concept", "title": "Classification and normalization"}, {"id": "1ffe1450-71ff-4eb2-8f66-d23d95afbf0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Expert knowledge"}, {"id": "380b8b23-6606-411f-aee6-1f9ba75e0937", "type": "concept", "title": "Subject positions"}], "description": "Discourse theory shows how categories and identities become thinkable, governable, and contested."}, {"id": "459b5859-edc5-44d5-b329-827edc70a4d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Knowledge, standpoint, and epistemic power", "children": [{"id": "ac366b8e-edf8-4780-a6a6-60dbb5deb211", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemic injustice"}, {"id": "8b322dda-1e67-4f38-9015-8670bcc735e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Situated knowledge"}, {"id": "2637bc35-b0bc-4d17-a65a-d862a6b6b6e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Counterpublics and alternative knowledge"}], "description": "Students examine how power shapes whose claims are trusted, archived, cited, or dismissed."}]}, {"id": "fd79269b-67ba-470d-be1c-dbe73caa1005", "type": "topic", "title": "Agency, Structure, Institutions, and Practice", "description": "One of sociology's enduring problems is how people act within conditions they did not choose. Students compare theoretical solutions to the agency-structure problem.", "children": [{"id": "e4bdc8a9-d59f-4d58-bd32-b65d6ff5570b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Structure and constraint", "children": [{"id": "d6b5b53c-e8ce-4cb7-a35e-eedfc7d43a33", "type": "concept", "title": "Rules and resources"}, {"id": "c523800f-b21e-4f85-b665-7cd76fcf127e", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional fields"}, {"id": "24606fdc-1684-4459-a880-043f2b583c3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Constraint and enablement"}], "description": "Students examine how institutions, class, race, gender, law, and organizations shape possibilities."}, {"id": "0096f57f-ec7e-41a8-b434-155cf2b631eb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Agency and meaning", "children": [{"id": "28e0330a-e550-4640-aced-66a9073e15cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical action"}, {"id": "d6135d52-bc20-4369-b7ac-f7f18d815d40", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity work"}, {"id": "6a2c5696-760d-4376-9b5a-900a0bcd897f", "type": "concept", "title": "Resistance and adaptation"}], "description": "Students analyze how people interpret, improvise, resist, and reproduce social arrangements."}, {"id": "23f3df41-0ba6-47e8-8cfb-d048e8a3898f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Practice theory and synthesis", "children": [{"id": "8cb66cdb-5c84-4d12-a65d-ea17ba89cf4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Giddens and structuration"}, {"id": "24a18424-e9ab-4c70-b6a5-7f238794724a", "type": "concept", "title": "Bourdieu and practice"}, {"id": "7f6d93f6-2f82-4de1-87b3-7542c882f6bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Routine, rupture, and transformation"}], "description": "Practice theory helps students connect dispositions, routines, material settings, and social change."}]}, {"id": "552bafc2-7e8d-4197-87c6-5272feb2c9e1", "type": "topic", "title": "Theory with Methods: Operationalizing Concepts", "description": "A theory course becomes stronger when students can connect concepts to research design. This topic teaches students to translate theory into questions, cases, evidence, and analytic strategies.", "children": [{"id": "3684a5da-c3b2-46c3-be7c-ba8958a40daf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "From concept to research question", "children": [{"id": "8be2a347-41c3-494e-9ee4-42efdea9412f", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept definition"}, {"id": "65a2f46c-d2c6-4182-a7ec-8b49602a1429", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope conditions"}, {"id": "8e7d6f05-882d-40ce-ab9f-56f1de249e2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Case selection"}], "description": "Students learn how theoretical concepts become researchable problems."}, {"id": "14585586-78e5-4693-a3e8-61f298af0903", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Operationalization and evidence", "children": [{"id": "b54505f2-332d-4784-9ab1-4214077679f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Indicators and dimensions"}, {"id": "59440118-12fa-4109-b45a-08f02d8bf24c", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualitative evidence"}, {"id": "5019eaa0-b2ac-4c92-b681-f6a6720f95d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative and historical evidence"}], "description": "Students identify what kind of evidence would support, complicate, or challenge a theory."}, {"id": "119e7a00-2fc1-401e-842a-b03cb7898733", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory-driven analysis", "children": [{"id": "772d9bc4-d838-47a6-b67e-459ab791d599", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytic memos"}, {"id": "cc7e0ca0-113e-43bb-9bca-c864f9fd16dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Competing explanations"}, {"id": "ee4a9888-5406-43b6-81a8-4cf2aa88cda1", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflexivity and limitations"}], "description": "Students practice applying theory without forcing evidence to fit."}]}, {"id": "17fc2a31-1822-40cd-a6b2-fff1bfdd1c67", "type": "topic", "title": "Applying Theory to Contemporary Social Problems", "description": "Students test theories against urgent social questions. The goal is not to pick one theory forever, but to learn which frameworks illuminate different dimensions of a problem.", "children": [{"id": "6d4f819a-66ad-4fe2-9bfb-7a8287ad4b2d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inequality and institutions", "children": [{"id": "a4b6d63f-2bbf-4cf1-8618-6820608c7dae", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional reproduction"}, {"id": "282c3421-f3ce-4388-8255-efab25390fa6", "type": "concept", "title": "Cumulative advantage"}, {"id": "89476ce4-c0ab-4aeb-9fdc-290fd88df1b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy and structural violence"}], "description": "Students apply theory to class, race, gender, education, health, housing, punishment, and work."}, {"id": "136cd67d-645d-4ec7-8f98-00c0bca026fa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media, technology, and culture", "children": [{"id": "c0211004-2947-4992-bd7a-4381648af2ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Platform capitalism"}, {"id": "9afb1ee4-b0af-4d52-9397-05cab5ffcd2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithmic power"}, {"id": "316e2d9b-4fcd-403e-8a33-ff072a5fa2eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Online self-presentation"}], "description": "Contemporary theory helps analyze platforms, surveillance, identity performance, misinformation, and digital labor."}, {"id": "15dd1ddc-6d16-43a3-b2a2-94365fdad398", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global crises and collective action", "children": [{"id": "111d0245-5361-4034-8213-005e1b854da6", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk and uncertainty"}, {"id": "eb018f3e-0cd0-492b-aab3-9d17f08f2535", "type": "concept", "title": "Movement framing"}, {"id": "9ec3d0d8-747d-464f-96bb-0c2183e5cbe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Global inequality and governance"}], "description": "Students use theory to examine climate, migration, nationalism, pandemics, and social movements."}]}, {"id": "0d184a75-f172-46c8-ae18-e1e3cb81dd34", "type": "topic", "title": "Theoretical Synthesis, Critique, and Seminar Paper", "description": "The final topic asks students to compare, synthesize, and critique theories in a sustained argument. They learn to write theory as a disciplined conversation rather than a list of summaries.", "children": [{"id": "5b208f11-c1b4-4b44-ab47-110ee9d71860", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparing theories", "children": [{"id": "becf2ec4-54a9-4e08-83aa-a5ef09a507b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Unit of analysis"}, {"id": "1ee4084b-c24c-42c7-8f92-90a2b74148f2", "type": "concept", "title": "View of power"}, {"id": "2838442e-a703-4561-b5e1-6878c4ad6339", "type": "concept", "title": "Model of social change"}], "description": "Students evaluate theories by their assumptions, strengths, blind spots, and explanatory reach."}, {"id": "633fc2ca-175a-415b-a339-1982f37dc27d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Building a theoretical framework", "children": [{"id": "689ef93d-d0c6-4715-916e-b0bf00a0ebd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Conceptual architecture"}, {"id": "0a4d4a21-38e3-47b9-8b4d-cabe1bc5d94e", "type": "concept", "title": "Compatibility and tension"}, {"id": "d2248a64-e9ff-4f9c-b224-ddde73b1deab", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope and limits"}], "description": "A framework combines concepts into a coherent lens for studying a problem."}, {"id": "e35ee1dd-c34f-4b4c-ad06-be4c6ac3ef45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Seminar paper design", "children": [{"id": "ef3b0f6d-974c-458f-9a90-3f7f300baf4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis and stakes"}, {"id": "64e364b7-b187-4c33-9c85-1745b329d7cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Literature positioning"}, {"id": "e0eff302-0dad-4241-84eb-452f4f7ca40d", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision, citation, and intellectual generosity"}], "description": "Students produce a polished argument that uses theory to explain or critique a contemporary issue."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$costjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'contemporary-social-theory'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Contemporary Social Theory',
  goal = 'Contemporary Social Theory',
  summary = 'This opening topic frames theory as a toolkit for explaining social order, conflict, meaning, power, identity, and change. Contemporary theory grows from classical debates while revising their blind spots. Functionalism asks how social institutions create order, integration, and stability; conflict theory centers domination, exploitation, contradiction, and struggle; interactionist theory studies meaning-making in everyday life; and phenomenology and ethnomethodology ask how ordinary social reality becomes taken for granted. Critical theory links social analysis to emancipation by examining domination in capitalism, culture, reason, and modern institutions. Feminist theory analyzes gender as a social structure, identity, embodied experience, and field of power, and essential frameworks for race, coloniality, identity, and power make contemporary theory more complete. Poststructural and postmodern theory destabilize assumptions about truth, subjectivity, representation, and power. Bourdieu offers one of the strongest bridges between structure and agency. Globalization theory studies cross-border flows, inequalities, institutions, identities, and risks. Network and rational-choice approaches model social relations, incentives, ties, and strategic action. Students compare how different theories explain domination and consent, how people act within conditions they did not choose, how concepts connect to research design, and how to test theories against urgent social questions, then compare, synthesize, and critique theories in a sustained argument.',
  data = $costjson${"slug": "contemporary-social-theory", "title": "Contemporary Social Theory", "description": "This opening topic frames theory as a toolkit for explaining social order, conflict, meaning, power, identity, and change. Contemporary theory grows from classical debates while revising their blind spots. Functionalism asks how social institutions create order, integration, and stability; conflict theory centers domination, exploitation, contradiction, and struggle; interactionist theory studies meaning-making in everyday life; and phenomenology and ethnomethodology ask how ordinary social reality becomes taken for granted. Critical theory links social analysis to emancipation by examining domination in capitalism, culture, reason, and modern institutions. Feminist theory analyzes gender as a social structure, identity, embodied experience, and field of power, and essential frameworks for race, coloniality, identity, and power make contemporary theory more complete. Poststructural and postmodern theory destabilize assumptions about truth, subjectivity, representation, and power. Bourdieu offers one of the strongest bridges between structure and agency. Globalization theory studies cross-border flows, inequalities, institutions, identities, and risks. Network and rational-choice approaches model social relations, incentives, ties, and strategic action. Students compare how different theories explain domination and consent, how people act within conditions they did not choose, how concepts connect to research design, and how to test theories against urgent social questions, then compare, synthesize, and critique theories in a sustained argument.", "topics": [{"id": "42dc4e1e-955c-49c3-a235-4a3b43228b85", "type": "topic", "title": "What Contemporary Social Theory Does", "description": "This opening topic frames theory as a toolkit for explaining social order, conflict, meaning, power, identity, and change. Students learn to read theory as argument, vocabulary, method, and critique.", "children": [{"id": "035529ab-4771-4f6f-8fda-bd985db7e3ca", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory as explanation and critique", "children": [{"id": "bb98fa86-a7d0-4a9b-aa6f-d74bf0c27800", "type": "concept", "title": "Concepts, mechanisms, and claims"}, {"id": "39746c1b-cdfc-4598-be42-1f78d5d92820", "type": "concept", "title": "Normative critique and empirical explanation"}, {"id": "a1af6ab2-9218-47df-9f86-15bb1d284005", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as a lens rather than an answer key"}], "description": "Students learn why theory matters for seeing social patterns that common sense often hides."}, {"id": "00ee27a6-32b5-4c1d-9b7c-a3fedff8304b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading difficult theory", "children": [{"id": "2a30a067-f079-4d47-b3fe-0e11a9439234", "type": "concept", "title": "Key terms and conceptual maps"}, {"id": "345fbd92-916e-4e10-8c80-bcda6ec5e657", "type": "concept", "title": "Argument reconstruction"}, {"id": "e4c583d9-8818-41e3-a9b2-73ae2b9d9a59", "type": "concept", "title": "Passage annotation and seminar questions"}], "description": "Theory becomes manageable when students learn how to track terms, arguments, assumptions, and stakes."}, {"id": "33afff2a-31df-47a7-8977-8ad285a6db0a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory and contemporary problems", "children": [{"id": "05516230-736e-4a16-b3f8-212d11f54de4", "type": "concept", "title": "Inequality, identity, and institutions"}, {"id": "fe93a1d4-cf22-4eea-9293-e2091490fc02", "type": "concept", "title": "Media, culture, and technology"}, {"id": "7289e55a-aac1-457d-be22-78a8d5c7fb1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Globalization, climate, and social change"}], "description": "Students connect abstract frameworks to concrete social issues."}]}, {"id": "ce2b4f28-36d5-41c9-9d69-bc38d6ba06c9", "type": "topic", "title": "Classical Foundations and Contemporary Problems", "description": "Contemporary theory grows from classical debates while revising their blind spots. This section links Marx, Durkheim, Weber, Simmel, Du Bois, Martineau, and Gilman to modern theoretical problems.", "children": [{"id": "c3f41393-ea6e-4354-81c6-14d53ee4444c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classical continuities", "children": [{"id": "edc5c207-f97e-41fd-9d67-3fe7621ddd01", "type": "concept", "title": "Marx and capitalism"}, {"id": "e129f39c-5999-4873-81dc-bf10b9ad1ed8", "type": "concept", "title": "Durkheim and social integration"}, {"id": "c394f0c1-d482-4d9d-bb96-c238edbb5605", "type": "concept", "title": "Weber and rationalization"}], "description": "Students see how contemporary theory inherits problems of capitalism, solidarity, rationalization, modernity, race, gender, and power."}, {"id": "13470aaf-4a73-4ba2-9a95-6d0555bd5ee0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Recovering excluded theorists", "children": [{"id": "ea8f87cb-618a-4235-84bd-18903e9cc3a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Du Bois and the color line"}, {"id": "5cea9c07-30f0-49bd-88e3-2173aecf9af5", "type": "concept", "title": "Martineau and gendered method"}, {"id": "9a30fb90-e3f3-4dae-8dbf-4c5ef4ac4d14", "type": "concept", "title": "Gilman and political economy of gender"}], "description": "A world-class theory course treats Du Bois, Martineau, Gilman, and other marginalized thinkers as central rather than supplemental."}, {"id": "d83c378a-499e-4497-ad07-8ce1593468ef", "type": "subtopic", "title": "From classical to contemporary", "children": [{"id": "38501f4a-9871-4f68-ad85-94a0a323891f", "type": "concept", "title": "Modernity and its critics"}, {"id": "3128982d-9e1b-4960-bffb-e1d76b0d3037", "type": "concept", "title": "Structure versus agency"}, {"id": "968412d6-d3de-4700-95a4-4740f2dfc37e", "type": "concept", "title": "Macro, meso, and micro levels"}], "description": "Students learn how later theorists keep, reject, or transform classical assumptions."}]}, {"id": "615ed7ac-33c2-4b35-b177-09c2209571f4", "type": "topic", "title": "Structural Functionalism and Social Systems", "description": "Functionalism asks how social institutions create order, integration, and stability. Students study both the power and limits of system-level explanation.", "children": [{"id": "dc7a58a7-a6c1-4721-8370-09903f5c5cc5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Parsons and social order", "children": [{"id": "ed080a24-78a5-456f-9d3d-453a76187ccb", "type": "concept", "title": "AGIL framework"}, {"id": "020f6dd3-0c33-4e0a-ad1c-e561e94e0d52", "type": "concept", "title": "Role expectations"}, {"id": "be360b0a-c2ce-430d-9f92-0ba6ebed49e2", "type": "concept", "title": "Value consensus"}], "description": "Parsons helps students understand society as interdependent systems of norms, roles, and institutions."}, {"id": "7d5bc6ff-98c4-47b4-bd3d-e314b54af46c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Merton and middle-range theory", "children": [{"id": "628c9ef2-d586-456c-9c72-8bffc90397a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Manifest and latent functions"}, {"id": "b0b45e49-c889-4e26-bef9-7f10217ce34e", "type": "concept", "title": "Dysfunctions"}, {"id": "725a9131-686d-4161-ad46-176ccb164494", "type": "concept", "title": "Middle-range explanation"}], "description": "Merton makes functional analysis more precise by focusing on unintended consequences and measurable social processes."}, {"id": "3c0c889a-ae21-41f9-9630-d61b73de2e16", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critiques of functionalism", "children": [{"id": "b54de1a5-4e96-40de-b0f3-f6078586179d", "type": "concept", "title": "Conservatism critique"}, {"id": "215a280f-b422-4c59-a75e-9948344206b5", "type": "concept", "title": "Power and inequality blind spots"}, {"id": "f996e41c-ed7f-46fa-9ff2-357b4416e47e", "type": "concept", "title": "Static versus dynamic models"}], "description": "Students learn why functionalism can understate conflict, domination, and historical change."}]}, {"id": "2ab69be3-b0c8-4d86-8a8d-6b7a659ffd56", "type": "topic", "title": "Conflict Theory and Neo-Marxism", "description": "Conflict theory centers domination, exploitation, contradiction, and struggle. Students learn to analyze capitalism, class, state power, ideology, and social change through neo-Marxist and critical traditions.", "children": [{"id": "a5e2d7fe-7880-4baf-948d-0085d6f633b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Capitalism and class power", "children": [{"id": "14e56898-02c0-4d2a-913e-6f00d791c63a", "type": "concept", "title": "Exploitation and surplus"}, {"id": "99a7675c-3b5a-47af-96fe-77172462b17b", "type": "concept", "title": "Class formation"}, {"id": "7bb328f8-6ae8-4a24-8d26-240cc4a983a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor, precarity, and commodification"}], "description": "Students revisit capitalism as a social relation, not just an economic system."}, {"id": "2b590db4-29c9-45b0-a22d-b9e35f355912", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Neo-Marxist revisions", "children": [{"id": "7332f6a4-4a49-4720-89ae-91de172c816d", "type": "concept", "title": "Gramsci and hegemony"}, {"id": "f0a04874-64da-4e56-93ba-fa9dcc9097a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Althusser and ideology"}, {"id": "e57311f0-c77b-4dc1-83ff-a05bdbeeb4ea", "type": "concept", "title": "World-systems and dependency thinking"}], "description": "Neo-Marxist theorists extend class analysis into culture, the state, ideology, and global capitalism."}, {"id": "fcd640f3-795c-41ca-843b-f48a0cc609f6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conflict beyond class reduction", "children": [{"id": "4a243dc0-4b09-4ab6-87a0-ca087256e0f0", "type": "concept", "title": "Racial capitalism"}, {"id": "ad479a67-3dd0-4e69-8b13-2fffc252ad63", "type": "concept", "title": "Social reproduction"}, {"id": "3a809887-7a80-417d-ae35-aa956f39d85f", "type": "concept", "title": "Ecological Marxism"}], "description": "Students examine how class interacts with race, gender, nation, empire, and environment."}]}, {"id": "1b3ddd92-4266-4965-bea5-05c4a9ccdb60", "type": "topic", "title": "Symbolic Interactionism and Dramaturgy", "description": "Interactionist theory studies meaning-making in everyday life. Students learn how selves, identities, situations, and social realities are produced through interaction.", "children": [{"id": "2e107e61-7776-4a5b-9b79-2dfedfb92c84", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mead and the social self", "children": [{"id": "ec2c654f-ee58-4e2a-9924-86ef628af7ab", "type": "concept", "title": "I and me"}, {"id": "490771eb-6ee5-427c-8eae-d4266e961b00", "type": "concept", "title": "Generalized other"}, {"id": "b05c6642-cfc6-4976-b3b6-d2eaa300c08b", "type": "concept", "title": "Mind, self, and society"}], "description": "Mead shows how the self emerges through communication, role-taking, and social interaction."}, {"id": "b286366e-459c-4850-96b2-71d41b138556", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Blumer and symbolic interactionism", "children": [{"id": "9332ad9f-a92e-4da3-8048-6aa4b5a06847", "type": "concept", "title": "Meaning, language, and interpretation"}, {"id": "7df0a07c-62d0-4dd9-b8b1-662f993c031c", "type": "concept", "title": "Joint action"}, {"id": "e3ab8fd8-a22d-4c23-9c52-9172cce97447", "type": "concept", "title": "Situational definitions"}], "description": "Blumer emphasizes that people act based on meanings formed and revised in interaction."}, {"id": "f5f45365-9b2b-42a0-b4cb-0a91ed1929d6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Goffman and dramaturgy", "children": [{"id": "913992de-aa96-481d-8299-c0735bf401a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Frontstage and backstage"}, {"id": "57692150-3d93-468c-8a51-860b3b839cfb", "type": "concept", "title": "Impression management"}, {"id": "d94ec48f-42ac-499d-b509-5eabf5d0ee2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Stigma and total institutions"}], "description": "Goffman gives students tools for analyzing performance, stigma, face-work, and institutional interaction."}]}, {"id": "409cc6e8-7827-4cf3-bd71-0d611a3fc465", "type": "topic", "title": "Phenomenology, Ethnomethodology, and Everyday Order", "description": "Phenomenology and ethnomethodology ask how ordinary social reality becomes taken for granted. Students study background knowledge, rules, interactional repair, and the production of everyday order.", "children": [{"id": "c80ad5b2-b946-4094-8d7f-66439a10c798", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Phenomenology of the social world", "children": [{"id": "21a8353f-e12e-4d0c-ae38-97ebe550b78a", "type": "concept", "title": "Lifeworld"}, {"id": "c53d2f64-15c2-49a7-875f-d25807b43573", "type": "concept", "title": "Typification"}, {"id": "39699486-8234-41e1-95de-2b10f27fdc4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Intersubjectivity"}], "description": "Students examine lived experience and common-sense knowledge as foundations of social reality."}, {"id": "8914af06-6ced-40fa-9f85-487a75ca1820", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethnomethodology", "children": [{"id": "b1e7f560-2530-4d5d-89b6-63b80f62071c", "type": "concept", "title": "Indexicality"}, {"id": "1530aed3-3b07-4f4e-baa5-96918008a782", "type": "concept", "title": "Accountability"}, {"id": "ca6d1b4d-20e1-4f86-a1c6-c39921ee339d", "type": "concept", "title": "Breaching experiments"}], "description": "Ethnomethodology reveals the hidden methods people use to make situations intelligible."}, {"id": "b021ca87-8087-4788-a928-92b52b4e13a3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Conversation and interaction order", "children": [{"id": "3505481e-55d7-4374-9067-1349286deb26", "type": "concept", "title": "Turn-taking"}, {"id": "901ef37f-945f-499d-a2be-15ecba6ef8ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Repair and sequence"}, {"id": "a24d4356-6a41-4b9a-9514-941f5e2bfa53", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional talk"}], "description": "Small-scale interaction becomes a window into norms, power, and institutional routines."}]}, {"id": "733d40b6-0ac1-4d6f-ad01-c7d11fb6b8f4", "type": "topic", "title": "Frankfurt School and Critical Theory", "description": "Critical theory links social analysis to emancipation by examining domination in capitalism, culture, reason, and modern institutions. Students learn to ask how society produces consent and constraint.", "children": [{"id": "b53be017-72cd-423c-ae3f-44dd6e57bf94", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early Frankfurt School", "children": [{"id": "b1b70303-eed5-480c-bbc1-b6da566baa69", "type": "concept", "title": "Horkheimer and Adorno"}, {"id": "9ae66bb3-03ce-44a4-9f50-879481792d08", "type": "concept", "title": "Culture industry"}, {"id": "aa824c3c-645f-47b4-9b26-ea5334c8050a", "type": "concept", "title": "Authoritarianism and mass society"}], "description": "Students study how theorists analyzed capitalism, culture, authority, and fascism."}, {"id": "e2366fc0-a913-4cf9-ae61-c474958d0a9d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reason, domination, and emancipation", "children": [{"id": "0d764598-7783-4e34-ab40-887d03f5b2a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Instrumental reason"}, {"id": "eddebc9f-b342-42d5-897c-21ca3c55ac8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Negative dialectics"}, {"id": "6b7830d7-f0cd-4e76-871a-38935d1f8ad0", "type": "concept", "title": "Immanent critique"}], "description": "Critical theory asks whether modern reason liberates people or becomes a tool of control."}, {"id": "bc852de1-142a-4f89-aead-9c4086d68940", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Habermas and communicative action", "children": [{"id": "19ea6297-6251-4231-b969-e1b3a8e52c48", "type": "concept", "title": "Lifeworld and system"}, {"id": "89974c6a-6af6-4720-98fd-93ecff497aaf", "type": "concept", "title": "Public sphere"}, {"id": "81730e96-6f46-4742-8f7f-a91f77f17b5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Discourse ethics"}], "description": "Habermas shifts attention to communication, public sphere, legitimacy, and democratic deliberation."}]}, {"id": "f5ad5db8-3d67-48f3-839d-3a34e07693b4", "type": "topic", "title": "Feminist Theory, Gender, and Sexuality", "description": "Feminist theory analyzes gender as a social structure, identity, embodied experience, and field of power. Students learn how feminist frameworks transform assumptions about knowledge, labor, family, sexuality, and inequality.", "children": [{"id": "e870ecc3-2125-4d77-bc59-870973cf26b3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist standpoint and epistemology", "children": [{"id": "0d05257d-0f80-4ca3-a831-7c9b2771ca4d", "type": "concept", "title": "Standpoint theory"}, {"id": "516e2150-806c-4019-b12a-ee86ba39d0af", "type": "concept", "title": "Situated knowledge"}, {"id": "088b9c0c-a606-48f5-9bfa-4ea3423abfc8", "type": "concept", "title": "Objectivity and reflexivity"}], "description": "Students examine how social location shapes what can be known and whose knowledge counts."}, {"id": "6a33cbf3-c457-4ba6-a9ee-7052b7a7cf64", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gender, patriarchy, and social reproduction", "children": [{"id": "bd98d200-8132-4852-8d81-e39d16f4f47d", "type": "concept", "title": "Care work"}, {"id": "5df4bb11-dc56-4376-ac15-701fe095867f", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered organizations"}, {"id": "21600d0e-4bc1-48b6-b37d-e653fbb58d24", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment and discipline"}], "description": "Feminist theory shows how unpaid labor, family, institutions, and bodies reproduce social order."}, {"id": "5580e55e-68d0-44e4-a5d6-1e1f1a91c94b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Queer and sexuality theory", "children": [{"id": "c6086b4d-6819-444e-9d2e-989ef49a6407", "type": "concept", "title": "Heteronormativity"}, {"id": "329b30b7-5065-4fd4-9bb0-17645336f645", "type": "concept", "title": "Performativity"}, {"id": "108a2f41-5bad-4878-be19-7073c9cf1804", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual citizenship"}], "description": "Queer theory challenges fixed categories and examines norms governing desire, identity, and legitimacy."}]}, {"id": "af87c0b9-0543-4b21-9790-2985e8318f28", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Race, Intersectionality, and Decolonial Theory", "description": "This topic adds essential frameworks for race, coloniality, identity, and power that make contemporary theory more complete. Students learn to analyze overlapping systems rather than isolated categories.", "children": [{"id": "38837697-6fa0-4e36-82eb-7f04ca5f61db", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critical race and racial formation", "children": [{"id": "651a3b9a-3333-440d-80ff-931d475b1995", "type": "concept", "title": "Racialization"}, {"id": "94537217-9c15-41b1-990b-082c63bdab82", "type": "concept", "title": "Colorblindness and racial ideology"}, {"id": "f8db10ab-dd97-4d80-844c-d0d909579e08", "type": "concept", "title": "Racial capitalism"}], "description": "Students examine race as historically produced, institutionally embedded, and politically contested."}, {"id": "da022bd5-d286-49e9-835a-8627778e7b86", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Intersectionality", "children": [{"id": "912d4c12-cab5-4027-9412-f08594738477", "type": "concept", "title": "Matrix of domination"}, {"id": "41c5f4b3-2abd-43a2-81c9-5ecac96e164c", "type": "concept", "title": "Interlocking oppression"}, {"id": "5ce848d5-5bb7-4a3e-93df-aff1e651cb27", "type": "concept", "title": "Patricia Hill Collins and Kimberle Crenshaw"}], "description": "Intersectionality studies how systems of power combine to shape lived experience and institutional outcomes."}, {"id": "186b52ef-147a-49db-bb45-65257030e48a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postcolonial and decolonial critique", "children": [{"id": "626d5ee7-3e1d-4c81-8536-a8d28d8da279", "type": "concept", "title": "Orientalism"}, {"id": "b7d4d0f1-e6ad-49e8-8afd-a1e2021eaa12", "type": "concept", "title": "Coloniality of power"}, {"id": "b38e46d6-699a-482c-b261-b0fdecf39a62", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemic justice and decolonizing knowledge"}], "description": "These frameworks challenge Eurocentric knowledge and examine colonial power after formal empire."}]}, {"id": "46b105e3-90ec-4132-bd74-ee30dd0e014d", "type": "topic", "title": "Poststructuralism and Postmodern Social Theory", "description": "Poststructural and postmodern theory destabilize assumptions about truth, subjectivity, representation, and power. Students learn the value and risks of theories that question grand narratives.", "children": [{"id": "c0f1f284-2b1d-4429-add8-78a16652a717", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Foucault on power and knowledge", "children": [{"id": "a59ccb3d-e2bc-4db2-8ac3-bbdf40ceb04a", "type": "concept", "title": "Discipline and surveillance"}, {"id": "65fd9f2e-506d-4875-b215-7f0bb30f65ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Biopower and governmentality"}, {"id": "87c313ea-bef0-47db-8788-5b08ee508892", "type": "concept", "title": "Genealogy and discourse"}], "description": "Foucault helps students analyze how institutions produce subjects, categories, and truths."}, {"id": "7470f504-7d65-4da3-9a61-4a83e74b0e8c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Baudrillard and simulation", "children": [{"id": "bb0a4a1b-7cb9-477e-a0b5-704379b7acf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Simulacra"}, {"id": "f006bd0f-9d9f-459d-b4f7-944a0590ccf9", "type": "concept", "title": "Consumer society"}, {"id": "a5654b8f-1a00-40e8-a2f9-d051685b80a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Media saturation"}], "description": "Baudrillard gives tools for thinking about media, signs, consumption, and hyperreality."}, {"id": "0db37144-feba-4685-82ac-dc8e6374c31e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Postmodern critique", "children": [{"id": "de2691d6-e93a-4beb-8f65-b90c936ca807", "type": "concept", "title": "Grand narratives"}, {"id": "cba1e76a-de14-4256-9aa6-a6829bed0198", "type": "concept", "title": "Fragmentation"}, {"id": "62d43b63-b5b3-4e52-be7d-c74b17580d33", "type": "concept", "title": "Relativism debates"}], "description": "Students evaluate critiques of universality, progress, objectivity, and stable identity."}]}, {"id": "6d95737f-8a3c-4a99-b25c-dd1e1188ba94", "type": "topic", "title": "Bourdieu: Habitus, Capital, Field, and Practice", "description": "Bourdieu offers one of the strongest bridges between structure and agency. Students learn how dispositions, resources, fields, and symbolic power reproduce inequality through ordinary practice.", "children": [{"id": "0eeae38a-5116-4953-b0a7-e1ae409ab05f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Habitus and practice", "children": [{"id": "d94b7d25-7893-4d24-a22f-652d7e9dedcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Disposition"}, {"id": "1095a4e6-99d7-4fcd-bb4f-e33ad9284376", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical mastery"}, {"id": "538578f5-bb40-4bec-abb3-3069c4818210", "type": "concept", "title": "Embodiment and taste"}], "description": "Habitus explains how social history becomes embodied as durable tendencies and practical sense."}, {"id": "f5478beb-ffe3-41a6-85a5-1cf1b73b2f9e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Forms of capital", "children": [{"id": "77df4972-80b4-4a13-b306-26ac5632574f", "type": "concept", "title": "Economic capital"}, {"id": "dd58e412-fa0c-47d8-ad3c-60fc90e3e9d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural and social capital"}, {"id": "69dfd9db-8578-41e2-9d0a-0dc61943a86f", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbolic capital"}], "description": "Bourdieu extends capital beyond money to show how advantage travels through culture, networks, and recognition."}, {"id": "163bba07-4eb1-4628-bd9d-84245910b878", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fields and symbolic power", "children": [{"id": "fcf21666-4e79-45c0-84f2-2f25efb7f619", "type": "concept", "title": "Autonomy and heteronomy"}, {"id": "75c52a04-1c75-484b-b0ee-b8ca6f8d214d", "type": "concept", "title": "Doxa and misrecognition"}, {"id": "27e8e2eb-10c8-4532-a696-59209c982fd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Education, art, and class reproduction"}], "description": "Fields help students analyze arenas of struggle with their own rules, stakes, and hierarchies."}]}, {"id": "6ced9f1e-9f31-4e50-88c3-8a7394cd9030", "type": "topic", "title": "Globalization, World-Systems, and Transnational Social Life", "description": "Globalization theory studies cross-border flows, inequalities, institutions, identities, and risks. Students learn to analyze how local life is shaped by global systems.", "children": [{"id": "f820cc33-2697-469f-9851-71db20809c85", "type": "subtopic", "title": "World-systems and global capitalism", "children": [{"id": "a0b594e5-32d4-4ef0-96b4-16e0444be76b", "type": "concept", "title": "Core-periphery hierarchy"}, {"id": "6ea48ac4-f9ee-4371-99a1-530509d95fa6", "type": "concept", "title": "Unequal exchange"}, {"id": "d425b794-3e2f-453b-a682-f76e255bff1f", "type": "concept", "title": "Dependency and development"}], "description": "Students examine core, periphery, and semi-periphery as relational positions in global political economy."}, {"id": "5b232a06-b7f6-45e7-a6cd-beee0a004086", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Transnationalism and migration", "children": [{"id": "4acff12c-18b3-4ba8-be34-6bacccc2a162", "type": "concept", "title": "Diaspora"}, {"id": "f8c3f42d-35f3-414f-b9de-2b2c0d680ada", "type": "concept", "title": "Global care chains"}, {"id": "9067b4e8-0dc0-43e7-b58d-4a5a3345120e", "type": "concept", "title": "Citizenship and belonging"}], "description": "Globalization is lived through movement, remittances, diasporas, and border regimes."}, {"id": "4f5faf4e-9e05-4bf2-b10f-d7881d81da85", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global culture and risk", "children": [{"id": "890cd8df-dcc4-4634-a70f-1c70186e0663", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural homogenization and hybridity"}, {"id": "1330ddc9-3684-4fdd-8f46-6a0915e9fdcc", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk society"}, {"id": "52e6d6c6-ec09-420c-8a9b-fbfe7948addd", "type": "concept", "title": "Global governance"}], "description": "Students analyze global media, institutions, climate, pandemics, and shared vulnerabilities."}]}, {"id": "1045a563-0692-4ebb-aebc-a425bb169e2c", "type": "topic", "title": "Network Theory, Rational Choice, and Social Capital", "description": "Network and rational-choice approaches model social relations, incentives, ties, and strategic action. Students learn where formal and relational theories clarify social life and where they oversimplify it.", "children": [{"id": "c720c262-19ff-4bc4-9375-8901b602da47", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social network analysis", "children": [{"id": "8e8d0f6f-179c-4cdf-b4ce-253ee7d0a56b", "type": "concept", "title": "Strong and weak ties"}, {"id": "8b428f37-f453-4a91-8fc3-ba6670ca302d", "type": "concept", "title": "Centrality and brokerage"}, {"id": "58be1206-fa78-4123-88a4-b2a1a748d9ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Diffusion and contagion"}], "description": "Networks show how position, ties, and structure shape opportunity and behavior."}, {"id": "57a9dbf1-921d-4b72-83b5-e9e2f0cac751", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Rational choice and exchange", "children": [{"id": "508f5186-a7c1-49bd-9e51-a57a8c6287eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Preferences and constraints"}, {"id": "4fbb6e1b-7507-426b-a090-d1e9106ff034", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective action"}, {"id": "41ea780d-0f23-4b30-8590-349df5c14674", "type": "concept", "title": "Game-like social situations"}], "description": "Rational-choice theory explains social action through incentives, constraints, and strategic decision-making."}, {"id": "f5982830-0fec-4e70-9514-8fdd72dee07f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social capital debates", "children": [{"id": "a22099b7-e0fb-4096-aa41-d2180ed88319", "type": "concept", "title": "Bonding and bridging capital"}, {"id": "6b046bd9-e315-4a76-b19e-96a74256aa97", "type": "concept", "title": "Trust and reciprocity"}, {"id": "14d90beb-ba07-4cb5-9c29-39023961c394", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of capital metaphors"}], "description": "Social capital links networks to trust, resources, inequality, and civic life."}]}, {"id": "a5803091-7657-42c1-9f22-70be661eb674", "type": "topic", "title": "Power, Ideology, Discourse, and Knowledge", "description": "This integrative topic helps students compare how different theories explain domination and consent. It connects Marxist ideology, Gramscian hegemony, Foucauldian discourse, feminist standpoint, and critical race epistemology.", "children": [{"id": "c11226f8-e50b-461e-a0c7-34fe6664a727", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ideology and hegemony", "children": [{"id": "a4c672ef-03b2-41be-86ee-516f492500d6", "type": "concept", "title": "False consciousness debates"}, {"id": "883ea566-bad8-458f-8c1e-d30cc09060e0", "type": "concept", "title": "Hegemony and common sense"}, {"id": "1ae139dd-ee8f-49d2-9872-428bc7c94501", "type": "concept", "title": "Media, education, and institutions"}], "description": "Students learn how domination can operate through common sense and consent, not only coercion."}, {"id": "b1bd621d-435b-48b5-886e-d003c5a73dac", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Discourse and subject formation", "children": [{"id": "4274c21c-c4d6-4bd0-8995-0c0e416a5617", "type": "concept", "title": "Classification and normalization"}, {"id": "1ffe1450-71ff-4eb2-8f66-d23d95afbf0f", "type": "concept", "title": "Expert knowledge"}, {"id": "380b8b23-6606-411f-aee6-1f9ba75e0937", "type": "concept", "title": "Subject positions"}], "description": "Discourse theory shows how categories and identities become thinkable, governable, and contested."}, {"id": "459b5859-edc5-44d5-b329-827edc70a4d7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Knowledge, standpoint, and epistemic power", "children": [{"id": "ac366b8e-edf8-4780-a6a6-60dbb5deb211", "type": "concept", "title": "Epistemic injustice"}, {"id": "8b322dda-1e67-4f38-9015-8670bcc735e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Situated knowledge"}, {"id": "2637bc35-b0bc-4d17-a65a-d862a6b6b6e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Counterpublics and alternative knowledge"}], "description": "Students examine how power shapes whose claims are trusted, archived, cited, or dismissed."}]}, {"id": "fd79269b-67ba-470d-be1c-dbe73caa1005", "type": "topic", "title": "Agency, Structure, Institutions, and Practice", "description": "One of sociology's enduring problems is how people act within conditions they did not choose. Students compare theoretical solutions to the agency-structure problem.", "children": [{"id": "e4bdc8a9-d59f-4d58-bd32-b65d6ff5570b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Structure and constraint", "children": [{"id": "d6b5b53c-e8ce-4cb7-a35e-eedfc7d43a33", "type": "concept", "title": "Rules and resources"}, {"id": "c523800f-b21e-4f85-b665-7cd76fcf127e", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional fields"}, {"id": "24606fdc-1684-4459-a880-043f2b583c3d", "type": "concept", "title": "Constraint and enablement"}], "description": "Students examine how institutions, class, race, gender, law, and organizations shape possibilities."}, {"id": "0096f57f-ec7e-41a8-b434-155cf2b631eb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Agency and meaning", "children": [{"id": "28e0330a-e550-4640-aced-66a9073e15cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Practical action"}, {"id": "d6135d52-bc20-4369-b7ac-f7f18d815d40", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity work"}, {"id": "6a2c5696-760d-4376-9b5a-900a0bcd897f", "type": "concept", "title": "Resistance and adaptation"}], "description": "Students analyze how people interpret, improvise, resist, and reproduce social arrangements."}, {"id": "23f3df41-0ba6-47e8-8cfb-d048e8a3898f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Practice theory and synthesis", "children": [{"id": "8cb66cdb-5c84-4d12-a65d-ea17ba89cf4f", "type": "concept", "title": "Giddens and structuration"}, {"id": "24a18424-e9ab-4c70-b6a5-7f238794724a", "type": "concept", "title": "Bourdieu and practice"}, {"id": "7f6d93f6-2f82-4de1-87b3-7542c882f6bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Routine, rupture, and transformation"}], "description": "Practice theory helps students connect dispositions, routines, material settings, and social change."}]}, {"id": "552bafc2-7e8d-4197-87c6-5272feb2c9e1", "type": "topic", "title": "Theory with Methods: Operationalizing Concepts", "description": "A theory course becomes stronger when students can connect concepts to research design. This topic teaches students to translate theory into questions, cases, evidence, and analytic strategies.", "children": [{"id": "3684a5da-c3b2-46c3-be7c-ba8958a40daf", "type": "subtopic", "title": "From concept to research question", "children": [{"id": "8be2a347-41c3-494e-9ee4-42efdea9412f", "type": "concept", "title": "Concept definition"}, {"id": "65a2f46c-d2c6-4182-a7ec-8b49602a1429", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope conditions"}, {"id": "8e7d6f05-882d-40ce-ab9f-56f1de249e2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Case selection"}], "description": "Students learn how theoretical concepts become researchable problems."}, {"id": "14585586-78e5-4693-a3e8-61f298af0903", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Operationalization and evidence", "children": [{"id": "b54505f2-332d-4784-9ab1-4214077679f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Indicators and dimensions"}, {"id": "59440118-12fa-4109-b45a-08f02d8bf24c", "type": "concept", "title": "Qualitative evidence"}, {"id": "5019eaa0-b2ac-4c92-b681-f6a6720f95d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative and historical evidence"}], "description": "Students identify what kind of evidence would support, complicate, or challenge a theory."}, {"id": "119e7a00-2fc1-401e-842a-b03cb7898733", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory-driven analysis", "children": [{"id": "772d9bc4-d838-47a6-b67e-459ab791d599", "type": "concept", "title": "Analytic memos"}, {"id": "cc7e0ca0-113e-43bb-9bca-c864f9fd16dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Competing explanations"}, {"id": "ee4a9888-5406-43b6-81a8-4cf2aa88cda1", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflexivity and limitations"}], "description": "Students practice applying theory without forcing evidence to fit."}]}, {"id": "17fc2a31-1822-40cd-a6b2-fff1bfdd1c67", "type": "topic", "title": "Applying Theory to Contemporary Social Problems", "description": "Students test theories against urgent social questions. The goal is not to pick one theory forever, but to learn which frameworks illuminate different dimensions of a problem.", "children": [{"id": "6d4f819a-66ad-4fe2-9bfb-7a8287ad4b2d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inequality and institutions", "children": [{"id": "a4b6d63f-2bbf-4cf1-8618-6820608c7dae", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional reproduction"}, {"id": "282c3421-f3ce-4388-8255-efab25390fa6", "type": "concept", "title": "Cumulative advantage"}, {"id": "89476ce4-c0ab-4aeb-9fdc-290fd88df1b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy and structural violence"}], "description": "Students apply theory to class, race, gender, education, health, housing, punishment, and work."}, {"id": "136cd67d-645d-4ec7-8f98-00c0bca026fa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media, technology, and culture", "children": [{"id": "c0211004-2947-4992-bd7a-4381648af2ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Platform capitalism"}, {"id": "9afb1ee4-b0af-4d52-9397-05cab5ffcd2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Algorithmic power"}, {"id": "316e2d9b-4fcd-403e-8a33-ff072a5fa2eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Online self-presentation"}], "description": "Contemporary theory helps analyze platforms, surveillance, identity performance, misinformation, and digital labor."}, {"id": "15dd1ddc-6d16-43a3-b2a2-94365fdad398", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global crises and collective action", "children": [{"id": "111d0245-5361-4034-8213-005e1b854da6", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk and uncertainty"}, {"id": "eb018f3e-0cd0-492b-aab3-9d17f08f2535", "type": "concept", "title": "Movement framing"}, {"id": "9ec3d0d8-747d-464f-96bb-0c2183e5cbe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Global inequality and governance"}], "description": "Students use theory to examine climate, migration, nationalism, pandemics, and social movements."}]}, {"id": "0d184a75-f172-46c8-ae18-e1e3cb81dd34", "type": "topic", "title": "Theoretical Synthesis, Critique, and Seminar Paper", "description": "The final topic asks students to compare, synthesize, and critique theories in a sustained argument. They learn to write theory as a disciplined conversation rather than a list of summaries.", "children": [{"id": "5b208f11-c1b4-4b44-ab47-110ee9d71860", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Comparing theories", "children": [{"id": "becf2ec4-54a9-4e08-83aa-a5ef09a507b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Unit of analysis"}, {"id": "1ee4084b-c24c-42c7-8f92-90a2b74148f2", "type": "concept", "title": "View of power"}, {"id": "2838442e-a703-4561-b5e1-6878c4ad6339", "type": "concept", "title": "Model of social change"}], "description": "Students evaluate theories by their assumptions, strengths, blind spots, and explanatory reach."}, {"id": "633fc2ca-175a-415b-a339-1982f37dc27d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Building a theoretical framework", "children": [{"id": "689ef93d-d0c6-4715-916e-b0bf00a0ebd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Conceptual architecture"}, {"id": "0a4d4a21-38e3-47b9-8b4d-cabe1bc5d94e", "type": "concept", "title": "Compatibility and tension"}, {"id": "d2248a64-e9ff-4f9c-b224-ddde73b1deab", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope and limits"}], "description": "A framework combines concepts into a coherent lens for studying a problem."}, {"id": "e35ee1dd-c34f-4b4c-ad06-be4c6ac3ef45", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Seminar paper design", "children": [{"id": "ef3b0f6d-974c-458f-9a90-3f7f300baf4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis and stakes"}, {"id": "64e364b7-b187-4c33-9c85-1745b329d7cc", "type": "concept", "title": "Literature positioning"}, {"id": "e0eff302-0dad-4241-84eb-452f4f7ca40d", "type": "concept", "title": "Revision, citation, and intellectual generosity"}], "description": "Students produce a polished argument that uses theory to explain or critique a contemporary issue."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$costjson$::jsonb,
  is_catalog = true,
